00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.370
So as I mentioned and
as Andrew mentioned,

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:52.030
we're a small team that is part
of a larger central engineering

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.580
group within CBS Interactive.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:59.050
So that means we work across
all of our digital brands.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.390
We started from
scratch on Google Cloud

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:05.810
January 2018 with no
systems to migrate.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.040
So it's sort of a wonderful
feeling in some ways.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.530
The expectation is that
we demonstrate our value

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.300
to the brands--

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.600
we meet their product
KPIs, for example--

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.000
and compete with other vendors.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.110
So outside competitors that come
into our brands and say, hey,

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:24.460
we offer this
brand this service.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:29.560
So we were able to integrate
with services for brands

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.400
like CNET and CBS All Access.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.490
So inspired by the nautical
theme of Kubernetes,

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.650
we embarked on our own voyage.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.480
So this theme of Kubernetes also
inspired our own product names.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.760
So you'll sort of see
those show up as well.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.480
So the first question was
really, where to start?

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.820
It's a classic
question, of course.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.340
And you can do so many
things in this space.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.830
You really can sort
of overwhelm yourself.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.800
So to go from zero
to production,

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.470
we had to consider our AI
business model, essentially,

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.380
which is the following thing.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:06.400
So opportunities.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.020
So what's the opportunity?

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.240
So consumer
expectations are very

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.260
high in terms of how
they interact with video.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.050
And so the business benefit
of building recommendations

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:19.040
is also very high.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.730
So we also saw a lot
of growth potential

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:24.280
in deepening our
own understanding

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.860
of our own content
across our brands

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:30.190
for the entire organization
and for our marketing teams,

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:34.940
product teams, sales,
search, ads, you name it.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.800
So we call this approach
content awareness.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.470
And our own
competitive advantage

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:45.640
is the data, the content, and
our own ability to get to it.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.070
And also with enriching
content understanding

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:55.370
and recommendations, we took
that as our starting point.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.120
And as I mentioned, the
barrier for data and content

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.890
was much lower for us.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:03.470
So we took advantage of that.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:04.780
So who are the consumers?

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.020
So who needs this?

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:08.390
Who needs these things that
we're trying to build out?

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:09.850
So one was internal teams.

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.260
First of all, starting
with ourselves.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:15.520
So sort of like a good chef,
you keep tasting your food

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.480
as you make it.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.010
So you'll see a little
bit of this later.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:24.790
Our consumer-facing customers
were our brands, essentially.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:31.130
Also, integrated marketing
teams and our ad teams as well.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.710
So for example,
our marketing teams

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.920
want to send relevant
materials to the right users

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:39.910
to let them know about the
latest show on All Access.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:42.580
So we can help them
make that happen.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.280
In terms of policy and process
for our business model,

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.210
if you will, we as
a team really needed

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.920
to orient ourselves
to Google Cloud.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:54.260
So a lot of us had not worked
on Google Cloud at that time.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:58.720
So we were learning new
technology as we went.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.790
We also-- some of
us were learning

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:04.540
principles of distributed
computing, even basic DevOps.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.670
We're still amateurs at
DevOps, but we have to do it.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:13.710
And getting our own
CI/CD service out.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:15.750
So we also needed
to sort of reorient

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.990
our partners, the people
we work with internally

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.330
to deploying living
features, as I call them,

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.250
that require a level
of experimentation

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.840
to be successful.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.330
So then how do we build it?

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.730
So applied is in our name.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:33.120
So we essentially start with
Cloud AI building blocks

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.300
and do transfer learning.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.420
And our own success
criteria were twofold.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:40.170
One where our own product KPIs.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:44.280
So when we deliver a service to
a brand, what are their KPIs?

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.420
We try to match those.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.350
And then our own model
benchmarks for our own services

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.810
internally.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.800
So as I mentioned,
what's the motivation?

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.450
Well, we have a lot of
wonderful content to work with.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:04.870
So videos, articles, images,
like in image galleries,

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:06.500
podcasts.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:09.070
And we have access to
them from our brands.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.040
So in order to build a really
good recommendation service,

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:17.170
we needed to enrich what we
know about our own content.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:22.270
There's a little nod to our
new show, "The Twilight Zone."

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.880
So we have a little
sample of content

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.580
here to give you a
flavor of what we have.

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:31.510
Gaming, tech news,
news, entertainment.

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:33.835
A lot of events get covered
as well, live events.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.750
So back to how we build it.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.210
So with the Cloud
AI building blocks,

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:44.400
we essentially said,
what we're going

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:50.670
to do is we're going to use
their services to get us going.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.830
And then once we
get going, we'll

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:58.150
start to build our own in-house
solutions on top of it.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:07:59.940
So this essentially
comes down back

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.570
to sort of a classical
approach to transfer learning.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:07.800
So we viewed using Google Cloud
AI, like video intelligence,

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.770
vision, natural language,
as a type of transfer

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:13.980
learning where we're trying to
get achievable representations

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.780
in the model sense.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:18.968
Time to learn is reduced for
us with a very small team.

00:08:18.968 --> 00:08:20.760
And then it's going to
increase performance

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.350
for all of our
downstream services,

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:24.690
because we don't have
to think on that piece.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:27.090
Because it really didn't
make any sense for us

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.340
to start from scratch
with some of those models

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:32.730
that Google Cloud AI handles.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:34.010
So we took it from there.

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:40.372
So speaking of time, the proof
was in the production for us.

00:08:40.372 --> 00:08:42.289
So this is just a sample
of some of the things

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:44.090
that we delivered
on those timelines.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.890
So also, to give you a sense
of where we are right now,

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.570
here's a glance at what
the world looks like.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:58.030
So the values aren't
really as important for me,

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:00.120
but it's really seeing
what we're combining

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.120
across CBS Interactive.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:05.160
And it's really the fact that
we have a living system now

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:05.850
in production.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:09.010
And we're able to
code, deploy, manage,

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:12.210
and deliver with a group
of 7 and 1/2 people.

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:18.060
So let me dive in a little
bit to our product components.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:23.680
So as I said, we started
with this initial idea

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:26.500
of content awareness.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.930
So roughly speaking,
we divide our world

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:32.000
into three product
classes, or components.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.890
So first is content awareness.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:37.710
So this is our service that
interacts with the Cloud AI

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:42.070
APIs, video intelligence,
vision, et cetera,

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:45.370
as well as data from our own
content management systems

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:48.670
and other important data APIs.

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:53.000
The second area of product is
what we call video services.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.380
So these are built on top of
the first service, content

00:09:56.380 --> 00:09:57.640
awareness.

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:01.810
So for an example, automated
captioning for our video

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:07.240
on demand, processing
live streaming as well,

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.410
doing object detection
on live streams,

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:14.620
bounding box, and entities
as well, automated clips,

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:17.860
thumbnails, and story adjusting.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:19.150
And we'll talk a little bit.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.300
And I'll show you a few little
examples if all goes well

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:24.560
in the video piece.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:26.060
And then the third
class, of course,

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.960
is recommendations and search,
which Rob will talk more about.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:35.310
So how do we architect for
all these products solutions,

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.040
especially starting
from zero and kind

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:41.790
of going as you go and implement
all the Cloud AI products?

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.310
So what I have here
for you is what

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:45.880
I like to call a
functional infrastructure.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:47.630
And I call it functional,
because I really

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:51.350
mix architecture and services
for the purpose of showing you

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:54.000
how we process content.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.840
So a lot of our other components
aren't represented here,

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:59.490
but I wanted to make
it easy to read.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:02.180
So working from the bottom up
is a way to look at this setup

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:03.420
here.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.140
So let me explain a little
bit about the color scheme.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:09.060
So the blue layer
are the media icons,

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.710
which obviously are the pieces
of content that do not live

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:15.530
within our own team system.

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:18.320
The pink layers
are on Kubernetes.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:19.820
The green-- excuse me.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:22.340
The purple layers is
our storage layer.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:26.180
The green layer on top are the
Cloud endpoints, so our APIs.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:29.420
The gray layer is a messaging
queue and monitoring.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:34.580
And the dotted box, of course,
represents the Cloud AI

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:36.140
services that we use.

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:38.930
And I'm also showing that we
make heavy use of the Service

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:41.130
Management API as well.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.430
So let me walk you through
a really quick example

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:45.330
of how this might work.

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.690
So an editor might
finalize a video

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:50.960
for encoding in our
content management system.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:55.430
So Trawler here on the bottom
will find that piece of content

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:57.680
and say, hey, this is new.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:00.020
Send that off to
our content workers

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:04.590
to start the job of interacting
with the Cloud AI services.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.470
And then bring back
the information around.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:08.060
Is it ready yet?

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:12.330
And also starting to
store off the results.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.800
We also then will use
Pub/Sub to emit messages

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.020
to our other services to
say, hey, this little bit

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.060
of information is now ready.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:22.120
Go do your thing.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:24.740
And a little bit of a
infrastructure call-out

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:27.710
for Kubernetes, as it really
aligns with our design

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:32.540
principles of immutability
of services and declarative

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:34.850
configuration for our
system, and allows

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:36.890
us to grow and scale quickly.

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:41.670
So let me dive a
little bit further

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:44.400
into the content awareness
architecture, where

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.350
we're using video intelligence.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:52.890
So going from left to
right here, what we do,

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:56.730
obviously, is we're tapping into
our various video feeds built

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:59.310
by our own video tech teams.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:02.070
So we'll handle a couple
of different formats;

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:08.460
video on demand and live stream
often in the form of HLS.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:11.460
And then we also will handle
when our content management

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:15.270
systems have updates
or depublish something.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:19.630
So that's our DataOps
bit, as I call it.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.090
And then we'll
track the operation

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:23.130
via the Service Management API.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:27.090
It's very important when you
have longer video content

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:30.720
to really make heavy use of
the long-running operation

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:33.210
to understand when
that process is

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:38.190
going to be complete so that
systems aren't left waiting.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:41.730
And then what we'll do
is we'll gather what

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:43.770
has been called by the service.

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:47.310
So that might include for video
intelligence getting the shot

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:52.470
change information, segment
level labels, frame level

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:54.918
information, and
entities if needed.

00:13:54.918 --> 00:13:55.710
Whatever is needed.

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:01.390
So what we do--

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:05.590
so in my Where to Start
section, I mentioned customers.

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:09.330
So one way we address this need
to make our work come alive

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:11.080
is in a web app.

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:13.810
So this is where we're
eating our own dog food.

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:17.280
So all the APIs we've
built, we put it

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:18.995
all into our own web app.

00:14:18.995 --> 00:14:20.370
So what you're
really seeing here

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:24.100
is the output of some of the
services that I just mentioned.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.440
So we have the video on
the Aston Martin Red Bull

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.840
simulator, racing simulator.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:34.080
And as it steps through,
the shot changes

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:35.790
have been detected.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:40.800
It updates the top
level shot entities

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:42.258
that we've also detected.

00:14:42.258 --> 00:14:43.800
And on the right-hand
side there, you

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:46.260
see three little
tiny video clips.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:48.720
Those are the output of
one of our video services,

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:52.440
which I'll show you a little
bit more about in a minute.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:54.450
And you also see, of
course, the output

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.820
of the speech
transcription, where

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:59.250
we've done a lot of
extra post-processing

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:04.530
to make the captions actually
look and feel really nice.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:14.280
So let me go on to one of the
video services I mentioned.

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:16.170
So this one we call Splice.

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:22.080
So let's say we have a video
about a Mars helicopter, which

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:24.450
is pretty compelling to me.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:27.960
So what we do is we
treat all of our videos

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.380
as essentially a type
of narrative discourse.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:31.850
So they're all
some sort of story.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:34.300
It doesn't matter
where it comes from.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:37.740
And then we'll break that down
into its foreground information

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:40.590
and its background information.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:42.330
And foreground and
background has a lot

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:44.610
to do with how a
narrative goes forward

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:47.010
and how we understand
narratives.

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:50.160
So let's say we have
this video clip here.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:54.130
This is what the whole
thing looks like.

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:58.810
And WTF, by the way,
means Who's The Future.

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:02.910
So don't think I'm
trying to fool anybody.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:07.140
So you get a sense here.

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:07.950
This is the clip.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:16.630
So now we'll have to take a look
at what Splice is looking at.

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:18.840
So Rob, if you could
play that first one.

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:22.180
You can play the Splice too.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.040
So what you have here
on the right-hand side

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:26.920
is the output of
our Splice algorithm

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:29.210
gives you the top
three candidates

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:30.940
of what we would
consider to be the most

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:34.910
engaging five-second
clips from this video.

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:36.910
So what we'll do--

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:39.790
what we're charting for you
in our own web application

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:40.750
is the output.

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:43.120
Also, the scoring
for information

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:45.760
gain on the foregrounding
and backgrounding,

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:49.330
and other detail information
from the video itself.

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:52.150
And then these clips, these
cue points, if you will,

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:55.450
will get sent back
into our video system

00:16:55.450 --> 00:17:00.150
to be picked up and
then used by the brands.

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:02.780
So as you can see
here, there's often

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:05.680
a lot of action
that gets captured,

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:09.589
often a lot of gist information
around what the video is about.

00:17:09.589 --> 00:17:12.140
And that's the goal of
an animated thumbnail.

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:16.109
Essentially, it's to
capture your interest.

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:18.319
So now I'll turn it
over to Rob to go

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:25.095
into a little more detail about
our hybrid recommender system.

00:17:25.095 --> 00:17:26.220
ROB HARRIGAN: Thanks, Adam.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:27.200
So I'm Rob Harrigan.

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:29.492
I'm a senior machine learning
engineer with the Applied

00:17:29.492 --> 00:17:31.130
Machine Learning Group at CBSI.

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:33.680
And my focus is on
recommendations.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:36.260
So I want to dig into two
of our services today.

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:38.930
And those services are GenReco,
which is our Generalized

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:40.875
Recommender, and Admiral.

00:17:40.875 --> 00:17:43.250
And now he's obviously the
leader of the recommendations.

00:17:43.250 --> 00:17:46.450
He tells which
recommendation to go where.

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:48.320
So a little bit of motivation.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:52.610
The goal of this is we want
baseline recommendations

00:17:52.610 --> 00:17:56.240
for any brands that we're
ingesting and analyzing content

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:56.960
for.

00:17:56.960 --> 00:17:59.000
So we have this great
content awareness built

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:02.550
on these Cloud AI API outputs.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:05.465
And we want to be able to
generate some baseline content

00:18:05.465 --> 00:18:08.030
to content recommendations.

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:11.060
And the fact that this is a
content to content similarity

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:13.730
recommender is a really--

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.130
it's a decision that
we made purposefully.

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:17.990
And that has a
lot of motivation.

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:21.020
One of which is that our brands
are content generation sites.

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:23.870
And so they're used
to delivering content.

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:26.900
And often, personalization
can add its own issues

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:28.490
with caching and delivery.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:30.140
And we don't want
to introduce any

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:33.350
of that problem for a
baseline recommender.

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:36.720
Also, our recommendations
should be content agnostic.

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.470
We want to be able to take all
the different types of content;

00:18:39.470 --> 00:18:42.987
images, image galleries,
reviews, articles,

00:18:42.987 --> 00:18:44.570
all these different
pieces of content,

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.500
and recommend them
for each other.

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:48.410
They're all about some topics.

00:18:48.410 --> 00:18:52.110
We can find which ones are
similar and recommend them.

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.260
And so we really
had three pillars

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:58.170
that we wanted to focus on
when we built this platform.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:01.370
The first is flexibility,
like the Duck boat,

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:05.250
or as Adam might say,
the Schwimmwagen.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:06.860
And so like a Duck
boat, we need to be

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:08.690
able to be on land and in water.

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:10.460
We need to be able
to handle lots

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:13.860
of rapidly changing business
logic with a small team.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:15.740
We need to be
configuration-driven and be

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:19.370
able to select models
quickly and accurately based

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.260
on whatever the brand's needs.

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:26.360
Second, we need to be
powerful, like the Ferrari.

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:29.780
New content has to be
recommended quickly and slowly.

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.390
We have news cycles
that are very fast.

00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:34.010
Think CBS News.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:37.490
And we've got content that
has very slow news cycles,

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:38.960
like an iPhone review on CNET.

00:19:42.340 --> 00:19:45.100
So this API will also
go on the front end.

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:46.915
And so it needs to
be fast and scalable.

00:19:46.915 --> 00:19:48.638
It needs to be performant.

00:19:48.638 --> 00:19:50.180
And we need to
support combinatorics.

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:52.013
We need to be able to
combine different sets

00:19:52.013 --> 00:19:56.930
of recommendations together
dynamically at request time.

00:19:56.930 --> 00:19:58.760
Lastly, it needs to be reliable.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:01.990
We need to also be
a '90 Civic, right?

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:05.290
Since this is going on
sites' front ends directly,

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:06.490
we can't go down.

00:20:06.490 --> 00:20:08.050
And again, we have
amateur DevOps.

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:09.260
We have a small team.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:12.280
We need high availability
without a lot of DevOps.

00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:15.430
So we want a separation of
powers between recommendation

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:17.350
creation and serving.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:19.570
We want those two
to be separated

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:22.690
so that there's little overlap.

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:24.220
So first, flexibility.

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:27.670
By flexibility, I mean
deciding which recommendations

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:31.690
to choose or not choose based
on customers' business rules

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:32.950
and business logic.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:36.040
They're going to give us rules
like users with five page views

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:38.260
today shouldn't get
trending, right?

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:41.020
Or everyone who lives in Poland
should get this recommendation

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:42.890
or not this recommendation.

00:20:42.890 --> 00:20:46.060
And we fully expect these
rules to be complicated

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:47.860
and rapidly changing.

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:51.100
And we need to support whatever
the business can tell us

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.110
about a user, a
piece of content.

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:54.910
Any requests they
make to us, we need

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:57.082
to be able to make
decisions based on that.

00:20:57.082 --> 00:20:58.540
And again, as a
small team, we need

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.330
to be able to make these easily
in a configuration-driven

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:04.000
environment so that
we can iterate rapidly

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:05.840
and don't have any downtime.

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:08.200
So we utilize YAML language.

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:10.627
And here's an example over
here under the Duck boat.

00:21:10.627 --> 00:21:12.460
So you can see we've
got three recommenders.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:16.150
And so the first recommender
would only be valid for users

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:18.700
that the site or the brand
tells us has at least three page

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:20.750
views and is authenticated.

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:23.540
The second one only valid for
users with at least five page

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:24.040
views.

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:26.080
Notice they don't have
to be authenticated.

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:29.500
The third one is for somebody
who really loves that site.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:31.750
But the point is that these
variables can be anything

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:36.970
that the brand can tell us and
pass into us at request time.

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:39.200
The second pillar is power.

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:40.900
We need to be like the Ferrari.

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:42.940
And this really refers
to two different goals,

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:44.210
which work in harmony.

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:47.170
The first is that we need to
combine models dynamically

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:48.610
at requests time.

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:51.700
And the second is the rapid
ingestion of new content.

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:54.760
We need speed in our
time to recommendation.

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:57.580
So besides just being able
to select models, which

00:21:57.580 --> 00:21:59.390
is what I talked
about in flexibility,

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:01.390
we want to build to
combine and backfill

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:05.010
models dynamically and in a
configuration-driven manner

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:06.850
at request time.

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:08.890
Support for combining
recommendations

00:22:08.890 --> 00:22:11.200
really simplifies our
back end, because it

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:13.420
means that we don't
have to pre-compute

00:22:13.420 --> 00:22:15.070
all possible combinations.

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:18.490
If a user is interested
in articles and video

00:22:18.490 --> 00:22:20.950
on one of our
brands, say CNET, we

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:24.520
don't have to compute an article
and video recommendation set.

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:26.950
We can combine the article
recommendations and video

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:28.973
recommendations.

00:22:28.973 --> 00:22:30.640
And this helps with
our speed of ingest,

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:33.040
because there's less things
that we need to pre-compute.

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:35.710
It also keeps our storage down.

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:37.090
And so obviously,
speed of ingest

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:38.632
is important, because
time that we're

00:22:38.632 --> 00:22:41.650
generating recommendations,
those pieces of content

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:43.060
are not being recommended.

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:45.970
If you think about the
lifecycle of a piece of content

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.850
on one of our brands, like CBS
News, it could be very short.

00:22:48.850 --> 00:22:51.610
And if we're taking hours
to generate recommendations,

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:53.020
we could completely
miss the time

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:56.270
where that piece of
content was relevant.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:22:59.500
So we have support for a
number of combination types,

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:00.920
all dynamically at request time.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:01.900
So the first is order.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:04.990
So return this GenReco
model, and then a fallback.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:06.880
Some say trending or something.

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:08.440
The second would
be an interleave.

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:10.450
So we're going to
alternate recommendations.

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:13.420
Or in the case of more
than two, round robin.

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:14.780
The third would be score.

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:17.597
So we have the model
scores stored in there.

00:23:17.597 --> 00:23:19.180
So if the model
scores are comparable,

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:22.925
we can combine those by the
actual similarity score.

00:23:22.925 --> 00:23:23.800
And lastly is weight.

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:28.120
So this would be, I want to take
this subset of recommendations,

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:30.700
but maybe order them by how
popular they are at the time

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:32.800
or something, right?

00:23:32.800 --> 00:23:35.440
And so over here on the right,
again, we have an example.

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:38.100
So you can see that the class,
this is a Redis recommender.

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:39.730
And underneath in
these args, we can

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:42.760
see that we've got two data
sources coming from Redis.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:44.560
So we've got a GenReco
model, which takes

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:46.840
input of site and content type.

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:48.730
And we've got a
fallback recommender.

00:23:48.730 --> 00:23:51.190
And you can see that one
parameter sort of interleave

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:53.530
means that we're going to
return a GenReco, and then

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:56.140
a fallback, and then a
GenReco, and then a fallback.

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:57.640
But we can change
that one parameter

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:00.310
to order to return
GenReco and then fallback,

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:05.460
or score, to order them by
their similarity scores.

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:07.140
The last pillar is reliability.

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:11.310
Reliability is crucial for any
production recommendation API.

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:14.520
Our challenge is that our
team has limited resources

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:18.190
with only a few team members
and no dedicated DevOps staff.

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:20.640
And machine learning
iterates fastest and works

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:24.210
best in an environment
where experimentation

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:26.850
and collaboration is encouraged.

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:31.260
That oftentimes does not
coalesce well with stability.

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:33.660
So our engineers and
our data scientists

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:37.410
need to feel free to
experiment on back end systems.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:40.218
Not too free, but pretty free.

00:24:40.218 --> 00:24:41.760
And these recommendation
models, they

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:45.150
need to be able to create new
ones without fear of breaking

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:47.490
what's already working, right?

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:49.740
So this means that where we
create our recommendations

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:52.073
in the back end and where we
serve them in the front end

00:24:52.073 --> 00:24:54.360
should be distinctly
different systems.

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:56.490
And you can work on
one and not the other.

00:24:56.490 --> 00:25:00.820
And we can update business
logic on one and not the other.

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:01.960
And so this is done.

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:04.140
We should serve the
best recommendations

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:06.468
at any given time.

00:25:06.468 --> 00:25:08.010
We're going to update
recommendations

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:09.110
as fast as we can.

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:10.950
And we give out the
best recommendations

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:13.312
that we know of at that time.

00:25:13.312 --> 00:25:15.270
And Google Cloud Endpoints
helps us a lot here,

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:17.340
because it handles
authentication,

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:20.790
monitoring, as well as giving
us a central place for API

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:23.340
documentation, which you
can see here on the right.

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:26.610
That's our portal, which is
part of Google Cloud Endpoints.

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:30.360
And it takes your OpenAPI spec
and it gives some nice Swagger

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:31.541
documentation.

00:25:34.130 --> 00:25:36.550
So let's take a look
at the architecture

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:41.380
and how this enables or
supports our three pillars.

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:44.200
So reliability really starts
with Admiral all the way

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:45.230
on the left.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:48.100
You can see that the only
piece of our infrastructure

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:50.380
between our customer
and Redis, which

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:53.320
is where we store our
recommendations, is Admiral.

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:55.900
It controls what
models are selected

00:25:55.900 --> 00:25:57.910
and what recommendations
are returned.

00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:01.660
And it is the only choke
point for reliability.

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:03.975
Our ingest process, as
Adam has already showed you

00:26:03.975 --> 00:26:05.350
a little bit,
starts with Trawler

00:26:05.350 --> 00:26:08.320
all the way on the right, where
we source data into the content

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:09.490
awareness system.

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:15.040
And then we augment it with
the Cloud AI APIs and store it.

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:18.230
And then GenReco is
really three cron jobs--

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:20.200
remember, we're
amateur DevOps here--

00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:23.030
and an auto-scaling
worker service.

00:26:23.030 --> 00:26:26.627
So I'm going to go through
these steps in a logical order.

00:26:26.627 --> 00:26:28.960
But it's important to remember
that in production, these

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:30.670
are all happening
simultaneously,

00:26:30.670 --> 00:26:34.090
so that we can get content
in as fast as possible.

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:36.670
But the first step up top
there is data collector.

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:39.040
And so data collector
sources data

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:41.080
from our content
awareness system,

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:45.040
which is augmented with the
output of the Cloud AI APIs.

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:49.300
And it stores it into GenReco,
and formats it in a way

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:51.400
that we can use it.

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:54.400
GenReco submit is what
we call the next step.

00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:57.580
And so if we tried to just
compare every piece of content

00:26:57.580 --> 00:27:00.022
that we get with every
other piece of content,

00:27:00.022 --> 00:27:01.480
it would be an
intractable problem.

00:27:01.480 --> 00:27:03.310
It would require
lots of resources.

00:27:03.310 --> 00:27:05.810
And most of those
comparisons would be useless.

00:27:05.810 --> 00:27:08.440
So this first step is
a filter to identify.

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:11.350
Based on some
initial processing,

00:27:11.350 --> 00:27:14.110
we identify the content that's
most likely to be recommended

00:27:14.110 --> 00:27:15.700
for any given piece of content.

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:20.020
And then we submit those as jobs
to our auto-scaling workers.

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:23.770
Those workers then compute
the actual similarity metrics

00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:25.990
based on our models.

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:29.190
And they can scale up when
content creation is high,

00:27:29.190 --> 00:27:32.230
and scale down when there's
no content coming in.

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:33.830
The last step is
the most important.

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:35.300
And it's what we call GenReco.

00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:37.943
And that's where we actually
create the recommendations.

00:27:37.943 --> 00:27:39.610
So we pull all the
comparisons that have

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:41.003
been computed by the workers.

00:27:41.003 --> 00:27:42.670
And we generate the
best recommendations

00:27:42.670 --> 00:27:43.840
that we have at the time.

00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:45.632
And again, this is all
running in parallel.

00:27:45.632 --> 00:27:47.470
And at any given
time, we're just

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:50.720
getting the best recommendations
that we have at that time.

00:27:50.720 --> 00:27:52.240
So how does this meet our goals?

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:57.970
It's flexible, because Admiral
can just serve JSON blobs.

00:27:57.970 --> 00:28:01.890
We can change business
logic configuration easily

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:04.090
in Admiral.

00:28:04.090 --> 00:28:06.680
It's powerful, because we
combine recommendations

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:08.300
in all these different manners.

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:11.950
We can have interleaving,
score, order, weight.

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:15.010
And all we have to do
is have models in Redis.

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:17.660
And we can combine
them dynamically.

00:28:17.660 --> 00:28:20.230
We don't have to compute
every possible combination

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:22.450
that a customer might be
interested in, because we

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:25.990
have these other assets
that we can use to build it.

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:28.690
And this means that ingest
happens as fast as possible.

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:31.240
Content is constantly moving
through this pipeline,

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:34.000
and we're generating
some recommendations

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:35.530
as fast as we can.

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:38.200
And then we're continually
updating those recommendations

00:28:38.200 --> 00:28:41.770
to make sure they're the best
that we know of at that time.

00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:42.910
Reliability.

00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:47.830
If any of these cron jobs goes
down, misses a run, breaks,

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:48.865
nothing happens.

00:28:48.865 --> 00:28:49.840
Not exactly nothing.

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:50.740
Something happens.

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:53.170
But it's not that big of a deal.

00:28:53.170 --> 00:28:56.230
We don't update recommendations
for those five minutes.

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:58.480
We have this buffer
time as engineers

00:28:58.480 --> 00:29:00.730
to work on this back
end system where

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:02.650
it won't affect any
of our customers

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:06.760
who are being served
recommendations.

00:29:06.760 --> 00:29:08.260
And so this
low-pressure environment

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:09.880
is really great for
our ML engineers

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:13.250
to be able to iterate rapidly
and create new models.

00:29:13.250 --> 00:29:16.210
So you might be wondering,
boy, that's a lot of boxes.

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:17.870
Does it actually work?

00:29:17.870 --> 00:29:19.000
I'm happy to say, yes.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.100
In multiple tests,
we've shown an increase

00:29:21.100 --> 00:29:24.500
in customer engagement of 14%.

00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:26.890
So now I'm going to
hand it back to Adam

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:31.500
for the conclusions
and future directions.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:34.110
ADAM LEARY: Thank you, Rob.

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:39.360
So before I kind of go into our
summary and future directions,

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:41.100
I hope that you
are starting to get

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:45.540
a flavor of the types of systems
on Google Cloud in general

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:48.750
that we're starting to use
and some of the decisions

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:51.540
that we made along the
way, which, of course,

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:55.810
we're always going
to improve as well.

00:29:55.810 --> 00:29:57.210
But what I would like--

00:29:57.210 --> 00:29:59.440
so we've sort of taken you
through our own stories,

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:00.820
so to speak.

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:03.480
So let's take a look a little
bit at some of the business

00:30:03.480 --> 00:30:06.660
impact we're seeing as
we progress as a team

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:09.020
and what are some of
our next areas of work.

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:15.470
So what has really emerged
from our initial work

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:17.750
is a few things.

00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:22.790
So visibility into our
own content across brands.

00:30:22.790 --> 00:30:26.570
So what I mean by that is the
enrichment of the information

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:32.060
that we get back from Cloud
AI services on our content.

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:34.850
And this has also spurred
some new areas for us,

00:30:34.850 --> 00:30:38.270
like we're now able to
search across videos, images,

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:41.000
all those entity objects.

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:43.400
In turn, this
visibility is beginning

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:46.310
to address sort of a pent
up demand, as I call it,

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:49.700
from our marketing teams,
to be able to rely on some

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:52.460
of these internal solutions.

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:54.320
And also what's
really important to me

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:57.590
is that we're now a
consideration for when features

00:30:57.590 --> 00:31:00.950
are being developed for our
content management systems

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:04.220
and our video tech,
and our video players.

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:07.550
So our fellow teams have been
very supportive in our journey

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:11.000
and have been very strong
advocates of our progress

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:12.452
as well.

00:31:12.452 --> 00:31:13.910
So at the onset,
I mentioned we are

00:31:13.910 --> 00:31:15.500
tasked with competing
head to head

00:31:15.500 --> 00:31:19.100
with a lot of outside companies
that come to CBS Interactive

00:31:19.100 --> 00:31:23.935
and say, hey, let's do a PFC. So
we might be demoing their work

00:31:23.935 --> 00:31:24.810
with their own brand.

00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:26.900
So this is one of
the motivators for us

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:29.840
to have our own web
application as well,

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:32.450
to sort of do our own sales
pitches to these brands

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:32.970
as well.

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:34.610
And then say, hey,
we're happy to go

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:39.350
into a test with our
services and their services.

00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:41.300
And we have done that.

00:31:41.300 --> 00:31:44.420
So we're also able now to
provide some strategic options

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:45.560
for the company.

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:50.893
And in many cases, we
can do it much cheaper.

00:31:50.893 --> 00:31:52.310
And so finally,
we're here to help

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:55.250
CBS Interactive as a
whole sort of embrace

00:31:55.250 --> 00:31:58.490
the possibilities
of using ML and AI,

00:31:58.490 --> 00:32:01.520
because it really is, as you
know if you work in the area,

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:04.110
it requires a bit
of a mind shift.

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:06.710
And so we also are aiming
to produce some tool kits

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:10.280
and packages that can be reused
by our other data science teams

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:14.300
and BI teams across the
company so that they reduce

00:32:14.300 --> 00:32:16.000
their amount of initial work.

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:21.570
So what are we going
to look at next?

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:24.330
What are we doing now and
what are we working on?

00:32:24.330 --> 00:32:28.800
So some of the things that we're
working on now and this year

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:29.620
are the following.

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:32.490
So I mentioned earlier in the
talk, video live streaming

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:33.880
enhancements.

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:37.620
So we are working on providing
some interesting features

00:32:37.620 --> 00:32:39.800
on top of live streams.

00:32:39.800 --> 00:32:41.440
So coming soon.

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:44.700
Second is we're in the
process of implementing

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:48.960
Kubeflow for our machine
learning pipeline management.

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:50.820
We've really reached
the maturity point

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:54.390
where we need to sort
of GitOps our pipelines

00:32:54.390 --> 00:32:57.660
and allow them to be reusable
by our ML engineering team,

00:32:57.660 --> 00:33:00.750
and even possibly other
data science teams.

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:02.910
Another area that's
really important for us

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:05.250
that we're exploring is GANs--

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:08.220
so Generative
Adversarial Networks--

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:10.770
for recommendation simulation.

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:13.710
So since we're sitting as
a central team among all

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:16.110
these brands, we're
often ready to go,

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:18.810
and we're waiting for the brand
to do their implementation

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:20.160
of our API.

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:24.150
So instead of just having that
be wasted downtime, what we'll

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:28.090
do is we'll create some GANs
that simulate user behaviors,

00:33:28.090 --> 00:33:33.900
have it hit our own APIs, and
continue to tune the models.

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:36.080
We're also working
on stacked embeddings

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:40.140
within the hybrid recommender
that Rob went over.

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:43.290
So one of the great ways
that it was designed

00:33:43.290 --> 00:33:46.870
is it allows us to add many
different, what we call, flows.

00:33:46.870 --> 00:33:48.300
And so each of
those flows can be

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:50.160
a different stacked embedding.

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:52.320
And we're starting to
do our own benchmarking

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:55.020
against other systems
so that we can continue

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:59.340
to monitor its
behavior and meeting

00:33:59.340 --> 00:34:02.850
our own expectations, as well
as the product expectations.

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:06.840
And then another area
that we are working with

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:12.030
is using AutoML Video and
AutoML Entity Extraction

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:14.975
for harnessing some of the
organizational knowledge.

00:34:14.975 --> 00:34:16.600
And let me explain
what I mean by that.

00:34:16.600 --> 00:34:20.010
So for AutoML Video,
we'll use that

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:22.469
for a secondary customization.

00:34:22.469 --> 00:34:27.449
So as we get information in
from the initial Cloud AI,

00:34:27.449 --> 00:34:30.870
like video intelligence
labels at the frame level,

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:33.360
and get the web
entities, we'll often

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:35.670
need to tune them for
certain brand use cases,

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:39.239
because it's a particular
person or brand

00:34:39.239 --> 00:34:42.659
name might be very rare and
hasn't really been seen.

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:44.530
So it gets mislabeled.

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:46.920
So we'll use AutoML
Video to sort of do

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:50.190
a secondary
post-processing update.

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:52.350
And then we'll inject
that back into our system

00:34:52.350 --> 00:34:54.300
to keep improving it.

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:56.219
And with the entity
extraction, that's

00:34:56.219 --> 00:35:00.030
also going to have a similar
function within our systems.

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:03.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

